<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/foundational/rock-cycle">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Rocks are not forever — they are <em>temporary forms</em> in a planetary recycling loop. The
    same material can be igneous, sedimentary and metamorphic across geologic time. Three great
    classes mark the three states of the loop: <strong>igneous</strong> (frozen magma),
    <strong>sedimentary</strong> (assembled debris), <strong>metamorphic</strong> (transformed
    under heat and pressure).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the loop as steps — scrub it like a cell dividing, but at mountain-belt pace" widget="timeline" config={rockTimeline} />

  <h2>Two engines drive the wheel</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Earth's internal heat</strong> melts, lifts and deforms — powering volcanism, subduction and mountain belts.</li>
    <li><strong>Sunlight (via weather)</strong> weathers and erodes, carries the debris to the sea, and buries it. The rock cycle is half geology, half solar power.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Reading a landscape</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Igneous</strong>: hard, crystalline, no layers — granite cliffs, basalt columns (Giant's Causeway).</li>
    <li><strong>Sedimentary</strong>: layered, often fossil-bearing, tells you it was once a seabed — the white cliffs of Dover are chalk from countless marine shells.</li>
    <li><strong>Metamorphic</strong>: banded, streaked, sparkly — slate roofs, marble statues. The bands are the pressure record.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The oldest known rocks on Earth (Acasta gneiss, ~4.0 billion years) are metamorphic — the
    cycle's survivors. Minerals recycle; the atoms in your next sandstone were once mountain
    tops.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> USGS "The Rock Cycle"; OpenStax, An Introduction to Geology.</p>
</TopicPage>
